steps.body:
An iterator is a small object that points at one element of a container, much like a pointer. Every container hands out two:

- `v.begin()` points at the first element.
- `v.end()` points **one past** the last element. It marks the stop and holds no element, so never write `*v.end()`.

`*it` reads (or writes) the element and `++it` moves to the next one. Loop until you reach `end()`:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {3, 5, 8};
    {{code}}
    for (int x : v) {
        std::cout << x << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
for (std::vector<int>::iterator it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
    *it *= 2;
}
```

Spelling out `std::vector<int>::iterator` is long, so people write `auto it = v.begin()`. It also matters for correctness: a `const` container (or a `const&` parameter) hands out a `const_iterator` that can read but not write, and a spelled-out `iterator` will not compile there. `auto` picks the right type. `v.cbegin()` and `v.cend()` ask for a `const_iterator` on purpose.

A `std::string` has the same `begin()` and `end()`, and `*it` is a `char`. Use `it != v.end()` as the condition: `!=` works for every container, while `<` only works for iterators that can jump. Compare `it != v.begin()` to put a space before every element but the first.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

void printAll(const std::vector<std::string>& colors) {
    // colors is const, so begin() gives a const_iterator: auto picks it
    for (auto it = colors.begin(); it != colors.end(); ++it) {
        std::cout << *it << '\n';
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums = {3, 5, 8};
    for (std::vector<int>::iterator it = nums.begin(); it != nums.end(); ++it) {
        *it *= 2;  // writes through the iterator into the vector
    }
    for (auto it = nums.begin(); it != nums.end(); ++it) {
        if (it != nums.begin()) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << *it;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';

    const std::vector<std::string> colors = {"red", "green", "blue"};
    printAll(colors);

    const std::string lang = "C++";
    for (auto it = lang.begin(); it != lang.end(); ++it) {
        if (it != lang.begin()) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << *it;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}

