#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int total(const std::vector<int>& v) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
        sum += *it;
    }
    return sum;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums = {4, 9, 1, 6};
    for (std::vector<int>::iterator it = nums.begin(); it != nums.end(); ++it) {
        *it += 1;
    }
    std::cout << total(nums) << '\n';

    const std::string code = "code";
    for (auto it = code.begin(); it != code.end(); ++it) {
        std::cout << '[' << *it << ']';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
Vector and string iterators can jump, not just step. `v.begin() + i` points at index `i`, and subtracting two iterators gives the distance between them, so `it - v.begin()` turns an iterator back into an index. `std::distance(v.begin(), it)` from `<iterator>` gives the same number and works on every container, even ones that cannot jump (there it counts one step at a time).

`std::find(first, last, value)` from `<algorithm>` scans the range in order and returns an iterator to the first match. When nothing matches it returns `last`, which is usually `end()`. Always check before you read:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> v = {7, 42, 3};
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
auto it = std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), 42);
if (it != v.end()) {
    std::cout << (it - v.begin()) << '\n';
}
```

> Gotcha: when the value is missing, `*it` dereferences `end()`, which is undefined behavior. Test against `end()` first.

`rbegin()` and `rend()` are reverse iterators. `rbegin()` points at the last element, `++` moves toward the front, and `rend()` sits one before the first. The loop looks exactly like a forward loop, so printing backwards needs no index math.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::string> fruits = {"apple", "banana", "kiwi", "mango"};
    std::cout << "second: " << *(fruits.begin() + 1) << '\n';

    auto it = std::find(fruits.begin(), fruits.end(), "kiwi");
    if (it != fruits.end()) {
        std::cout << "kiwi at index " << (it - fruits.begin()) << '\n';
    }

    auto missing = std::find(fruits.begin(), fruits.end(), "plum");
    if (missing == fruits.end()) {
        std::cout << "plum not found\n";
    }

    for (auto rit = fruits.rbegin(); rit != fruits.rend(); ++rit) {
        if (rit != fruits.rbegin()) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << *rit;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}

