steps.body:
Vectors insert and erase by iterator, not by index:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    const int i = 1;
    const int x = 15;
    const int a = 1;
    const int b = 3;
    {{code}}
    for (int x : v) {
        std::cout << x << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
v.insert(v.begin() + i, x);                // x lands at index i
v.erase(v.begin() + i);                    // remove index i
v.erase(v.begin() + a, v.begin() + b);     // remove indexes a to b - 1
```

A range is always half open: `first` is removed, `last` is not. So `v.erase(v.end() - 1)` removes the last element and `v.erase(v.end() - 2, v.end())` removes the last two.

**Memory:** a vector keeps its elements side by side, so every insert or erase shifts all the later elements to open or close the gap. That costs O(n). An insert can also outgrow the capacity and move everything to a new buffer. Either way, iterators at or after the change (or all of them, after a reallocation) are **invalidated** and must not be used again.

To erase while walking, use the iterator `erase` returns. It points at the element after the removed one, so you skip `++it` on that pass:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {4, -2, 7, -5, -1, 3};
    {{code}}
    for (int x : v) {
        std::cout << x << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end();) {
    if (*it < 0) {
        it = v.erase(it);
    } else {
        ++it;
    }
}
```

> Gotcha: `v.erase(it)` followed by `++it` uses a dead iterator. This loop is O(n) per erase on a vector. The next lesson shows the faster erase-remove idiom, and this loop is how you erase while walking a map or a set.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

void print(const std::vector<int>& v) {
    for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
        if (it != v.begin()) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << *it;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}

void print(const std::vector<std::string>& v) {
    for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
        if (it != v.begin()) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << *it;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> names = {"Ann", "Cid", "Dee", "Eve", "Fay"};
    names.insert(names.begin() + 1, "Bob");
    print(names);
    names.erase(names.begin() + 3);
    print(names);
    names.erase(names.begin(), names.begin() + 2);
    print(names);

    std::vector<int> nums = {4, -2, 7, -5, -1, 3};
    for (auto it = nums.begin(); it != nums.end();) {
        if (*it < 0) {
            it = nums.erase(it);  // it now points at the next element
        } else {
            ++it;
        }
    }
    print(nums);
    return 0;
}

